Imagine that we don't care about which people the people choose to shake hands with. If there were no restrictions, there would be three handshakes between the natural number 1 and the other numbers 2 to 9. I guess that this is just overcounting in gcd(9,3) = 3. Considering this, there are $\binom{9}{3} \cdot \frac{7!}{(7-3)!} / 3! = 362880 / 21 = 17280$ possibilities. However, not all of these can occur. <answer>Very good! Suppose that no two people shake hands with the same two people and there are three people who shake hands with exactly two other people. The three people must each shake hands with different people, so there are 8 choose 3, or 56 ways to choose three people among the group of 8 other people. Then there are 3! or 6 ways to arrange which three people with whom (each of the chosen three) shake hands with exactly two others. So $56 \cdot 6 = 336$ arrangements work. Very good! <answer>However, let's count all arrangements in which the group of nine people take two handshakes each pairwise. This can be done in the following way: for each pair of people, they have two options (being the people who shake hands). So there are $2^{\binom{9}{2}} = 33554432$ arrangements in total. Remarkably, $33248$ arrangements are subsumed in that same count. <answer>So the answer to this problem is $33248$, which is $248$ when considering it $\pmod{1000}$.\end{document}

